-- Lesson 1: What is a Ratio?
'{"hook":"Every time you double a recipe, read a map scale, or mix paint colors, you are using ratios — a concept so fundamental that Euclid devoted an entire book of the Elements to it 2,300 years ago.","inventor":{"name":"Euclid","birth_year":-325,"death_year":-265,"nationality":"Greek","bio":"Euclid of Alexandria wrote the Elements around 300 BCE, one of the most influential textbooks in history. Book V covers the theory of ratios and proportions, building on earlier work by Eudoxus of Cnidus."},"year_invented":-300,"etymology":{"word":"ratio","from":"Latin","parts":[{"root":"ratio","meaning":"reckoning, calculation, reason"},{"root":"reri","meaning":"to reckon, to think"}]},"story_paragraphs":["Long before formal mathematics existed, ancient traders needed to compare quantities. If 3 sheep were worth 2 goats, that relationship — 3 to 2 — was a ratio. Babylonian merchants used ratios in trade, Egyptian builders used them in construction, and every civilization that measured the world relied on them.","Euclid formalized the theory of ratios in Book V of the Elements (around 300 BCE), building on the work of Eudoxus. He defined when two ratios were equal — a surprisingly deep question that foreshadowed real number theory by over two thousand years.","The concept is simple: a ratio compares two quantities by division. Writing 3:2 or 3/2 both mean the same thing — for every 3 of one thing, there are 2 of another. But simple concepts can be incredibly powerful.","Today, ratios are everywhere: screen aspect ratios (16:9), financial ratios (price-to-earnings), gear ratios in cars, mixing ratios in chemistry and cooking, and map scales. Understanding ratios means understanding how quantities relate to each other."],"key_contributors":[{"name":"Eudoxus of Cnidus","contribution":"Developed the theory of proportions that Euclid later recorded in the Elements"},{"name":"Thales of Miletus","contribution":"Used ratios to calculate the height of the Great Pyramid from its shadow"}],"real_world_applications":[{"name":"Cooking","icon":"🍳","description":"Recipes are ratios: 2 cups flour to 1 cup sugar scales up or down proportionally"},{"name":"Maps","icon":"🗺️","description":"Map scales like 1:50,000 tell you how distances on paper relate to real distances"},{"name":"Screen Ratios","icon":"📱","description":"16:9 widescreen, 4:3 classic TV — the shape of every screen is defined by a ratio"},{"name":"Finance","icon":"💰","description":"Price-to-earnings ratio helps investors compare the value of different stocks"}],"image":{"url":null,"alt":"Page from Euclid''s Elements","attribution":"Wikimedia Commons, public domain","source_url":null}}'::jsonb)
